Fair value before die 2 is revealed: the four dice have expected values 7.0 (die 1), 7.625 (die 2), 3.67 (die 3) and 3.67 (die 4), so the expected sum is about 21.96. The sum's standard deviation is about 7.1, which is wide enough that informed counterparties can pick off quotes near fair value. Die 2 is revealed before stage 2, so stage 1 carries the most risk.

A rough expected-value check at alpha = 0.5 puts the best ask around 29–30 and the best bid around 15, where the noise-trader edge roughly covers the informed-trader cost. Both sides are about 7 from fair value, which is a reasonable spread here.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 29.0}

The public fair value for this round is about 21.96: the expected sum of the four dice, with die 1 = 7.0, die 2 = 7.625, and dice 3 and 4 = 3.67 each. Nothing is revealed yet.

Round 3 is the main evidence on alpha. It settled at 38, and three quotes that would have drawn an informed buyer (asks of 24.5, 30 and 30) saw no informed trade. That points toward low alpha, so I put most of the weight on alpha ≈ 0.05 with a smaller weight on 0.5, giving an effective alpha of about 0.11. Under pure noise trading, the spread that maximises expected profit is about ±4.5 around fair. Adding a small informed-trade cost at alpha ≈ 0.11 pushes it out to about ±5. That gives a bid of 17.0 and an ask of 27.0.

{"bid": 17.0, "ask": 27.0}

Die 2 is revealed as 6. The expected sum of the hidden dice is 7 (die 1) + 22/6 (die 3) + 22/6 (die 4) = 20.33, so the public fair value is about 20.3. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.1, close to the noise traders' 6.

I'm already long 1, so I'll quote an ask below my stage-1 level to offload the position, and a bid well under fair so I only add to the long if a seller clearly undervalues the contract.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 23.5}

The deck has 16 cards summing to 86, so the expected value of one card is 5.375 and the expected sum of 3 dealt cards is 16.125. The standard deviation of the sum is about 8.5, and noise traders have a standard deviation of 7.2 around the fair value.

Nothing new has been revealed, and the no-trade result on quote 1 says almost nothing about alpha, since a no-trade outcome is about equally likely under each alpha value. A rough expected-profit comparison across the three alpha values favours a wide spread over a tight one, because informed flow costs more as the quote tightens. The 8.1 / 24.2 quotes are about as good as anything I tested, so I'll keep them.

{"bid": 8.1, "ask": 24.2}

Fair value after card 1 = 6: the remaining 15 cards average 80/15 ≈ 5.33, so cards 2 and 3 are expected to add about 10.67, giving a fair value of about 16.7.

The quotes that drew no trades last stage (8.1/24.2) are close to optimal. Noise traders (std 7.2) make the outer quotes profitable, and informed flow is the main risk on tighter quotes. Using a posterior on alpha of roughly 0.46 on average, the bid side is best around 7.5 and the ask side is flat between about 24 and 27. I'm moving slightly to 7.5 / 25.0.

{"bid": 7.5, "ask": 25.0}

The fair value is 6 (card 1) plus the expected sum of cards 2 and 3. The remaining 15 cards sum to 80, so each averages 5.33 and the two unseen cards add about 10.67. That puts fair value at about 16.7.

Posterior weight on a high alpha is modest, since no trades occurred in three quotes. The best bid and ask come from balancing expected noise-trader profit against informed-trader losses. For the bid, this gives about 7.0, since a higher bid picks up informed sellers when the settlement is 7. For the ask, it gives about 25.0, which is the same as my previous ask and near the balance point.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 25.0}

Round 2 starts with nothing revealed, and the deck is reshuffled each round, so the public fair value is the mean of three cards drawn from the deck. The deck sums to 86 across 16 cards (mean 5.375), so the expected settlement is 3 × 5.375 ≈ 16.1. Round 1's outcome doesn't carry over.

Adverse selection is the main concern. With alpha averaging about 0.48 across the three equally likely values, an informed counterparty trades against any quote near fair value. A rough normal approximation (sd of the three-card sum ≈ 8.5) puts the ask-side optimum near 22 and the bid-side optimum symmetrically near 10. I'll quote around those levels and update once I see how counterparties trade.

{"bid": 10.1, "ask": 22.1}

The two hidden cards are unknown and the revealed card is 6, so the public fair value is about 6 + 2 × 5.33 ≈ 16.7. From the round 2 sales at my bids of 10.1 and 11 when the settlement was 7, the counterparties look mostly informed (posterior about 0.84 on their share).

Informed traders only hit my quotes when they're on the wrong side of the true value. The true value is 6 + (sum of two hidden cards), which ranges from 6 to 32. A bid of 6 and an ask of 32 sit at the edges of that range, so informed traders should never trade against them. Only noise traders can hit these quotes, and they add a small positive expected profit.

{"bid": 6, "ask": 32}

The contract is the sum of three cards dealt from the 16-card deck. The deck sums to 86, so the expected sum is 3 × 86/16 ≈ 16.1, and the standard deviation of the sum is about 8.5. Nothing about this round's cards has been revealed, and past rounds are independent of this one, so the public fair value is about 16.1.

The history hints that informed traders may be present: in round 3 someone bought at 18.6 before a settlement of 19, and in round 2 sellers hit our 10.1 and 11 bids before a settlement of 7. Given that, I'll quote a moderate spread around fair value rather than a tight one, to limit adverse selection from informed flow while still earning on noise traders.

{"bid": 12, "ask": 20}

With nothing revealed, the public fair value is the mean of three cards drawn from the deck: the deck sums to 86 across 16 cards, so the fair value is 3 × 86/16 ≈ 16.1. The settlement's standard deviation is about 8.5.

Against noise traders (valuation N(16.1, 7.2²)), a half-spread of about 10 maximises expected profit when I average over the alpha prior (mean ≈ 0.48). A tighter spread loses too much to informed flow, and a wider one gives up too much noise flow. So I'm quoting about ±10 around 16.1, rounded so the bid rounds down and the ask rounds up.

{"bid": 6.1, "ask": 26.2}

The fair value before any reveal is 16.125: the deck averages 86/16 = 5.375 per card, and three cards give 3 × 5.375.

The history points to a high alpha. Every sale to me at a bid settled below that bid (7 vs 10.1 and 11, 5 vs 12 and 8, 4 vs 6.1), which is what informed sellers would do. Informed flow picks off any quote on the wrong side of the settlement value. With the settlement value's spread (sd about 8.5) wider than the noise traders' edge, the best quotes sit near the extremes of the possible sums (0 to 39), where informed trades are almost impossible. A bid of 1 keeps informed sell risk to the single outcome V = 0, and an ask of 39 is never picked off by an informed buyer.

