Round 6 has no revealed cards yet, so the public fair value is the expected sum of three cards drawn without replacement from the 16-card deck. The deck sums to 86, so the mean card is 5.375 and the fair value is 3 × 5.375 ≈ 16.1. The standard deviation of the three-card sum is about 8.5.

A rough expected-profit check across the three possible alpha values, with the trade-conditional values estimated from this distribution, favours a fairly wide quote around 16. A bid of 8 and ask of 24 gives a positive average expectation across the three alpha values, while narrower quotes lose more to informed traders when alpha is 0.9.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 24.0}

The contract settles at max minus min of the two dice, which equals |D1 − D2|. Before any reveal, the fair value is E|D1 − D2| = 3.775.

To choose the half-spread, I weighed the noise-trader edge against adverse selection from informed traders. Alpha is unknown, with mean about 0.48, so I averaged expected profit over alpha ∈ {0.05, 0.5, 0.9}. A half-spread of about 3 gave the best expected profit. Rounding gives bid 0.7 (rounded down from 0.775) and ask 6.8 (rounded up from 6.775).

{"bid": 0.7, "ask": 6.8}

The revealed die 1 = 4 gives settlement S = |4 − d2|, which is 0 when d2 = 4. The sale to me at 0.7 in stage 1 is much more likely from an informed seller when S = 0, so the posterior puts about 89% on S = 0. The public fair value is therefore about 0.33, well below the 2.1 that the prior alone would suggest.

Since this is the last quote of the round, each trade's expected profit is S − bid for a seller to me, or ask − S for a buyer from me. The noise sellers' expected profit is maximised by a bid near −1, which avoids the informed sellers, who only arrive when S < bid. The noise buyers' expected profit is maximised by an ask near 2.7, where the informed buyers' losses (S = 3, 4, 5) are still modest.

{"bid": -1.0, "ask": 2.7}

The unrevealed fair value is E[|die1 − die2|] = 3.775. The noise traders' valuations centre there, so I quote around it.

Posterior on alpha after the no-trade at bid 1 / ask 6.9 is roughly 0.46 on average. Under that, the expected-profit trade-off comes out near bid 1.0 and ask 7.0. Bid 1.0 is the best grid point I checked on the bid side. On the ask side, 7.0 beats 6.9 and 7.1. The informed flow beyond 7 is mostly the die-1=9 with die-2=2 outcome, which is the tail the ask has to price against.

{"bid": 1.0, "ask": 7.0}

Card 1 = 10 leaves two unseen cards drawn from {1, 3, 3, 3, 3, 7, 7, 10, 10, 10}, so the prior fair value is 10 + 11.4 = 21.4. The earlier sell at our bid of 17 is evidence: a noise trader or an informed one sold at 17, which points to a low settlement. Updating over the alpha prior (mean about 0.48) puts the public fair value near 19.8, with a skewed posterior: about 0.34 on S = 14 or 16, and about 0.3 on S = 27 or 30.

Quoting against that posterior with alpha around 0.5, informed sellers hit a bid above 14 and informed buyers lift an ask below 27, which costs more than the noise flow earns. The best bid is 14.0 (expected edge about +0.17) and the best ask is 27.0 (about +0.02), so the quote is wide.

{"bid": 14.0, "ask": 27.0}

Fair value for the 3-card sum is 3 × 67/11 ≈ 18.3. Nothing has been revealed this round, and the no-trade result at quote 1 gives no new information about the round's cards.

Round 1 suggests informed traders are rare. A noise trader sold to us at 17, and the informed-trader-consistent quotes (ask 23.5 when the true sum was 27) drew no buyer. So alpha is probably near 0.05, with a posterior mean around 0.15. That means adverse selection is small, so I can quote fairly tightly around 18.3.

