With 3 cards from the deck, the expected sum is 67/11 × 3 ≈ 18.3. Card 1 is not revealed until stage 2, and the round-5 sale at 13 hints slightly lower, so I'm using a fair value around 18.

Across rounds 1 and 2, several asks below the settled value of 27 saw no buyers, which an informed trader would have taken. That makes a high alpha unlikely, so counterparties are mostly noise traders. For noise flow, the per-quote profit is maximised at roughly 0.75 standard deviations (about 2.8) from fair, so I'm quoting about 2.8 either side of 18.

{"bid": 15.2, "ask": 20.8}

Fair value before any reveal: die 1 averages 7.125, die 2 averages 3.8, and die 3 averages 5.25, so the public fair value is 16.175. The settlement's standard deviation is about 5.6.

Expected alpha is (0.05 + 0.5 + 0.9)/3 ≈ 0.48. Since expected profit is linear in alpha for a given quote, I can price with that mean. Balancing noise-trader spread capture against informed-trader adverse selection, a half-spread of about 6.5 is roughly optimal. Rounding bids down and asks up gives a bid of 9.6 and an ask of 22.7.

{"bid": 9.6, "ask": 22.7}

Fair value before any reveal is the sum of the die means: 7.125 + 3.8 + 5.25 = 16.2. The total has standard deviation about 5.7, so informed counterparties will trade against any quote whose far side is inside that range. Noise traders have valuation sd 4 around 16.2, so each noise fill earns roughly the distance from fair value.

Using the prior on alpha (mean about 0.48) and the fact that quote 1 saw no trade (weak evidence for lower alpha), expected profit per side peaks at about 4.5 from fair value. That puts the ask near 20.7 and the bid near 11.7. The curve is fairly flat, so these are approximate.

{"bid": 11.7, "ask": 20.7}

The stage-1 fair value is the expected sum of the three dice. Die 1 averages 7.125, die 2 averages 3.8, and die 3 averages 5.25, so the fair value is about 16.2. The round's standard deviation is about 5.7, and the noise traders' valuations have standard deviation 4.

From the history, the no-trade quotes where an informed buyer would have hit my ask (round 1 quotes 1-2, round 2 quote 4) make alpha = 0.9 unlikely. Alpha is probably 0.05 or 0.5, so I use an average alpha of roughly 0.3. Balancing noise-trader flow against adverse selection from informed traders gives a half-spread of about 4.2 around 16.2. That puts the bid near 11.9 and the ask near 20.5.

{"bid": 11.9, "ask": 20.5}

Public fair value is the expected sum before any reveal. Die 1 averages 57/8 = 7.125, die 2 averages 0.6·1 + 0.4·8 = 3.8, and die 3 averages 21/4 = 5.25, so the fair value is 16.175.

Quote 1 drew no trade, which carries little information. I'll keep the quote centred on fair value with a wide spread, since the sum is bimodal (roughly 13 or 20 depending on die 2) and the chance of informed trades is significant.

{"bid": 11.9, "ask": 20.5}

Under the stated model, the public fair value of the sum before any reveal is E[die 1] + E[die 2] + E[die 3] = 7.125 + 3.8 + 5.25 = 16.175. Since alpha is unknown, I average over its prior (mean about 0.483). Expected profit is linear in alpha for fixed quotes, so this average is the right input.

The informed counterparties know the exact sum, which ranges from 6 to 30. Noise traders value the contract around 16.175 with sd 4. Quoting near 22 has negative expected profit, because informed trades cost more than noise trades earn. Pushing the ask to 30 removes the informed buyers, since the sum cannot exceed 30. Pushing the bid to 6 does the same on the sell side. Both quotes still have small positive expected value from noise flow, so I quote both sides.

{"bid": 6.0, "ask": 30.0}

The contract is 5 × heads over 8 flips. The coin is 0.1 or 0.5 with equal probability, so the expected heads count is 2.4 and the expected settlement is 12. Round 1's result says nothing about this round's coin, which is redrawn, and nothing is revealed yet, so the public fair value is 12.

With noise traders centred on 12 and a settlement standard deviation of roughly 10, a half-spread of about 9 to 10 is near the best average outcome across the three possible values of alpha. A tighter quote loses heavily if alpha is 0.5 or 0.9, and a wider one gives up too much flow if alpha is 0.05.

{"bid": 3, "ask": 21}

Current estimate: the buy at 20.6 suggests the settlement is probably high. Before this quote, the counterparty types were roughly 47% informed at alpha=0.5 and 45% at alpha=0.9, with the 0.05 case at about 7%. Combining that with the buy gives an expected value near 22.7, and about 75% weight on V ≥ 25.

With the position at -1, I'm not willing to buy back near that value, so the bid is essentially worthless. I'll leave it at 0, where it only catches rare noise sellers. The ask is where expected profit peaks: near 34 to 35, informed buyers only take the trade if V is 35 or 40, while noise buyers still arrive. Expected gain from the ask is about +0.28 per unit.

{"bid": 0, "ask": 35}

No information about this round's coin or flips has been revealed yet, so the public fair value is the prior expectation: 5 × 8 × E[p] = 5 × 8 × 0.3 = 12.

I quoted symmetrically around 12. Past trades suggest a moderate chance that the counterparty is informed, so I widened the spread to about ±10 to cover adverse selection while still capturing noise-trader flow.

{"bid": 2, "ask": 22}

At quote 1 of round 2 nothing is revealed, so the public fair value is the prior mean: E[red] = 6, so E[settlement] = 60. Round 1 gives little information about alpha, so I use its prior mean, about 0.48.

For a candidate ask A, expected profit from the ask side is roughly (1 - alpha) x P(noise buys) x (A - 60) minus alpha x E[(V - A)+], where V = 10 x Binomial(10, 0.6). Scanning integer asks, this peaks near A = 80 (about +0.39 per quote). The bid side is similar, and the best bid is near 40 (about +0.31). Both are about 20 from fair value, which keeps the expected loss to informed traders small.

{"bid": 40, "ask": 80}

Fair value given draw 1 = red: E[R | draw 1 red] = E[R²]/E[R] = 6.4, so the public value is about 64. The two no-trade quotes this round and the round 1 history (no informed sale at bid 56 when the settlement was 40) suggest a low probability of an informed counterparty, but not zero. I estimate the public value at about 63.4.

With a noise-trader spread of 13 and informed flow around 16%, the expected profit per side is highest for a bid near 52 and an ask near 74. That is about 11 below and 11 above fair value.

{"bid": 52, "ask": 74}

Draw 1 was red, so the conditional mean settlement is about 64. Quotes 1–3 of this round drew no trades, which suggests the value is not at an extreme and makes informed traders less likely. The posterior mean settlement is about 63.8, and the posterior on alpha leans toward the low value (roughly 0.7 on 0.05, 0.27 on 0.5, 0.02 on 0.9).

This is the last quote of the round, so the position settles at the contract value right after it and there is no later inventory to manage. I chose the bid and ask that maximise expected profit against that mix of informed and noise counterparties. The ask near 74 and the bid near 53 come out as the expected-profit maximisers under this posterior.

{"bid": 53, "ask": 74}

Settlement is 10 × Binomial(10, 0.6), so the public fair value before any reveal is 60. Informed traders never crossed any past quote, so the history doesn't say much about alpha. The two noise-looking buys (at 72 and 74) do lean toward a lower alpha, so I'm using a rough posterior mean of about 0.28.

Expected profit from a quote is roughly (1 - alpha) × P(noise trade) × edge, minus alpha × the expected loss to informed traders. Balancing those gives an ask near 72 and a bid near 48. The bid sits further from 60 than the ask because the binomial distribution is skewed toward high values.

{"bid": 48, "ask": 72}

Given draw 1 was blue, the other 9 balls are i.i.d. red with probability 0.6, so the public fair value is 10 × 5.4 = 54. The noise-trader valuation is centred on 54 with sd 13, and the settlement value 10R has sd about 14.7.

Alpha is unknown, but its prior mean is about 0.48, and expected profit is linear in alpha, so I use that mean. Under that mix, the per-side expected profit is maximised around a bid of 40 and an ask of 70. Those levels are wide because informed flow is substantial and the settlement value is quite uncertain. Trades so far don't clearly shift the alpha estimate, so I keep the same prices.

{"bid": 40, "ask": 70}

The posterior fair value is about 74 (prior fair 60, then 64 after draw 1 was red, then pushed up by the buy at 70 and the buy at 74.5). Informed buyers only take the ask when the settlement is high, so buys carry much of the signal.

Pricing each side against the mixed informed/noise arrival, the expected profit from a sale to us peaks near a bid of 60. The expected profit from a purchase from us peaks near an ask of 90, since noise buyers at high asks are rare and informed buyers only come in at V=100. The bid and ask are separate events because only one counterparty arrives, so I set each side independently.

{"bid": 60, "ask": 90}

Round 5 fair value is about 73, not 64. Both buys at 70 and 75 suggest informed buyers, which pushes the posterior toward high draw counts. Working through the trades in rounds 1-4 also points toward a low informed share, though this estimate is rough.

Settlement ≈ 10R, with R ≈ 1 + Bin(9, 0.6) before the latest trades. After the 70 and 75 buys, I put roughly 60% on R ≥ 8 and an expected value near 73. Within this quote, a bid around 63 buys from noise sellers at a discount to that value while limiting exposure to informed sellers, and an ask around 85 sells to noise buyers while limiting exposure to informed buyers. The expected gain is positive on both sides, with the bid slightly ahead of the ask.

{"bid": 63, "ask": 85}

Draw 1 was blue, so the other 9 balls are still iid 0.6 red and the prior fair value is 10 × 5.4 = 54. The buy at 71.5 in quote 1 is evidence that the value is high, since an informed buyer only buys when the value exceeds the ask. Earlier rounds, including the no-trade outcomes where an informed seller would have traded, point toward a moderate-to-high alpha. Weighting those, my posterior mean for the settlement is roughly 60 to 68.

I'm short 1, so buying back at a bid below the expected value is profitable. A bid near 50 is positive-EV across the plausible alpha values, with adverse selection from informed sellers limited at that level. Selling more at an ask near 80 is roughly break-even to slightly positive, since only a value of 90 would trade against it.

{"bid": 50, "ask": 80}

Revealed flips give 2 heads in 3. The posterior on the coin is P(p=0.2) ≈ 0.20 and P(p=0.5) ≈ 0.80, so flips 4 and 5 have expected heads ≈ 0.88. The fair value is about 2.88, and settlement S is 2, 3 or 4 with probabilities about 0.33, 0.46 and 0.21.

Informed traders buy above my ask when S exceeds it and sell below my bid when S falls under it. Noise traders trade when their valuation is beyond my quote. Averaging over the alpha prior, expected profit per side is maximised at the edges of the settlement support: an ask of 4.0 (informed traders never buy, since S ≤ 4) and a bid of 2.0 (informed traders never sell, since S ≥ 2). Both quotes still capture noise flow.

{"bid": 2.0, "ask": 4.0}

The three revealed flips show 2 heads, so the coin posterior is about 0.20 for p=0.2 and 0.80 for p=0.5. The expected settlement is about 2.88, and it can only be 2, 3 or 4.

Since this is the last quote of the round, any trade leaves a single unit of inventory that settles immediately. A bid of 2 can only be hit by noise traders, because the informed seller would need the settlement to be below 2, which is impossible. A bid above 2 starts to attract informed sellers when the settlement is 2, which costs more than the extra noise-trader flow earns. The same reasoning puts the ask at 4: the informed buyer needs a settlement above 4, which is impossible, and raising the ask to 4.1 or higher loses more noise-trader flow than it gains.

{"bid": 2.0, "ask": 4.0}

The public fair value before any flips is 5 × (0.5×0.2 + 0.5×0.5) = 1.75. Noise traders value the contract around 1.75 with SD 0.88, so they trade on both sides of a symmetric quote. Informed traders trade whenever the settlement lands outside my quotes, which costs me. Across the three possible alphas, a tight spread loses money to informed flow, and a wide spread gives up too little noise flow.

I checked a few spreads against the settlement distribution, averaging over alpha. The best expected value came from a bid of 0 and an ask near 3.0. A bid of 0 is useful because informed sellers can never sell to me there (settlement can't be below 0), so only noise sellers with valuations below 0 can hit it.

{"bid": 0, "ask": 3}

Before any reveal, the public fair value is 5 × 0.35 = 1.75. Noise traders value the contract around 1.75 with sd 0.88, and informed traders buy only above our ask or sell only below our bid.

Against that, a bid of 0 and an ask of 3 come out slightly positive in expectation over the three possible alphas. A bid of 0.5 is marginally worse because it gives informed traders a profitable sell when the settlement is 0. The ask of 3 is close to break-even on the sell side, and moving it either way makes the expectation worse.

{"bid": 0, "ask": 3}

I'd quote only the ask here. The expected settlement is about 9.45 (E[max] of the three dice, from the distribution of the largest face). Informed counterparties sell to me when V is above my ask and buy from me when V is below my bid. The value distribution is skewed toward low outcomes (many 0s), so informed sellers are costly at any bid I'd post. The bid side loses money in expectation at every price I checked, so I leave it off.

On the ask, the expected profit from noise buyers at 11.5 outweighs the expected loss to informed buyers, who only trade when the max is 12. That is roughly +0.1 per quote on average across the alpha prior. I'll update alpha and the public fair value as trades come in.

{"bid": null, "ask": 11.5}

The round has no revealed information yet, so the public fair value is the expected maximum of the three dice. Working through the CDF of the maximum over the breakpoints gives an expected value of about 9.45.

Because one of the counterparties may be informed, I'm quoting a spread of about 2 around that value. That is wide enough to limit adverse selection if alpha is 0.5 or 0.9, and it still lets noise-trader flow through when alpha is 0.05.

{"bid": 8.5, "ask": 10.5}

Die 3 = 11 is the largest possible value for die 3 and exceeds every face of die 1 (max 10) and die 2 (max 11). So the settlement is 11 for certain, and the public fair value is 11.

Since the value is known, my inventory has no risk: the +1 position settles at 11 whatever I do. Informed traders also know the value is 11, so they will only trade against quotes on the wrong side of 11. Quoting a bid below 11 and an ask above 11 keeps them away. The remaining question is how far to set the quotes from 11. Noise traders have valuations around 11 with standard deviation 1.8, so the expected edge from a bid at b is P(valuation < b) × (11 − b), and the mirror image holds for the ask. Maximizing this gives roughly 1.4 away from 11, so a bid of 9.6 and an ask of 12.4 (asks round up).

{"bid": 9.6, "ask": 12.4}

Die 3 is 11 and the other dice can't exceed 11 (die 1 tops out at 10, die 2 at 11), so the settlement is 11 for certain. Informed counterparties know this too, so they won't trade against quotes on the wrong side of 11. Any trade with a bid below 11 or an ask above 11 is profitable for me, and the only trades I'll get are from noise traders.

Noise valuations are Normal(11, 1.8²). Per side, the expected profit is roughly edge × P(noise trader trades at that edge). Maximizing edge × Φ(−edge/1.8) gives an edge near 1.35–1.4 on each side. Bid 9.6 and ask 12.4 are both about 1.4 from 11, which is close to that optimum.

